# 120 MHz on the CORE clock -- the constraint the 5.2/120 MHz target actually needs.
#
# 120 MHz IS reachable (mistakes-log #57).  Older revisions of this file said the
# opposite because they assumed the PLL output divider had to be a particular
# value; the output divider O is a free integer on the VCO:
#
#   27 x 58 / 13 = 120.4615 MHz, VCO = 27 x 58 = 1566 MHz (legal 1330..2133)
#   PFD = 27 MHz (legal >= 19)
#
# so the period written here is 8.3014 ns -- the ACTUAL clock period -- not the
# 8.3333 ns of a 120.000 MHz clock.  Writing 8.3333 would make "timing closed"
# 0.4% looser than the hardware, which is the kind of slack that made the old
# "45 MHz passed" claim wrong (see mistakes-log #54).
#
# Hardware chain: 27 MHz on D18 -> GTP_PPLL I=1/M=1/F=58/O0=13 -> core clock.
# The generated clock is declared on the PLL output pin, so the core is timed at
# exactly the hardware frequency regardless of what the board oscillator does.
create_clock -name board_clk -period 37.037037 -waveform {0 18.5185185} [get_ports {clk}]
create_clock -name core_clk -period 8.3014 -waveform {0 4.1507} [get_pins {u_core_pll/CLKOUT0}]
# No multicycle, no false path, no clock-group exception on CPU/cache/AXI paths.
